"<strong>The Counting view.</strong> Press <strong>Counting</strong> in the row of views at the top of the picture (beside Many nuclei, Alpha decay and Beta decay). A cesium {#} source sits in front of a Geiger tube, and the counter counts for the same <strong>Counting time Δt</strong> again and again.",
"In the strip: <strong>Play</strong> counts in real time (<strong>{#}×</strong> and <strong>{#}×</strong> run faster): each count flashes a track into the tube, and the tapes under the counter mark when each count came in the present Δt and the four before it. <strong>Count {#} times</strong> counts for {#} more Δt at once. <strong>Reset</strong> clears the counts and starts the random draws of this draw set again.",
"In the sidebar’s <strong>Counting</strong> section: the <strong>Count rate R</strong> slider (counts per second), the <strong>Counting time Δt</strong> ({#}, {#}, {#} or {#} s), then the readouts: <strong>Counting times n</strong>, <strong>Expected mean μ = RΔt</strong>, <strong>Mean count x̄</strong>, <strong>Standard deviation s</strong>, <strong>√x̄</strong>, <strong>s²/x̄</strong> and <strong>χ² against the Poisson</strong>, and a table of the counts against the Poisson distribution. Changing R or Δt clears the counts, since that is a new experiment.",
"Below the table: the <strong>Already waited t₀</strong> slider and the waiting time readouts: <strong>Mean wait</strong>, <strong>Expected mean wait {#}/R</strong>, <strong>Standard deviation of the waits</strong>, <strong>Waits longer than t₀</strong>, <strong>Expected share e^(−Rt₀)</strong> and <strong>Mean time still to wait after t₀</strong>.",
"<strong>Blind</strong> hides the Poisson, normal and exponential predictions. <strong>Record reading</strong> stores R, Δt, μ, n, x̄, s, √x̄ and s²/x̄; <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> fills Table {#}.",
"The source is long lived (cesium {#}, half life {#} years), so during the lab its activity does not change: the count rate R is constant. Each wait between counts is drawn from the exponential distribution with mean {#}/R, independently of the others. That is all the simulation does; the Poisson distribution of the counts follows from it.",
"The counter has no dead time and there is no background; real counters miss counts that come too close together and also count cosmic rays. The data are simulated, and seeded: the same steps from a fresh page give the same counts.",
"Find out how random counts behave: compare repeated counts from a steady source with the Poisson distribution, show that their standard deviation is the square root of their mean, compare the waits between counts with the exponential distribution, and see why a single count of N carries an uncertainty of \\(\\sqrt N\\).",
"<strong>Counts are Poisson.</strong> A radioactive source with a constant count rate R gives counts at random times, each one independent of the last. The number of counts X in a counting time Δt then follows the <strong>Poisson distribution</strong> with mean \\(\\mu = R\\,\\Delta t\\):",
"\\[P(X = k) = \\frac{\\mu^k e^{-\\mu}}{k!}, \\qquad k = 0, 1, 2, \\dots\\]",
"Its standard deviation is \\(\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\mu}\\): the variance equals the mean. So for a Poisson sample, \\(s^2/\\bar x\\) should be close to {#} and \\(s\\) close to \\(\\sqrt{\\bar x}\\). For a small mean the distribution is lopsided (it cannot go below {#}); for a large mean it is close to the <strong>normal distribution</strong> with the same mean and \\(\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\mu}\\).",
"<strong>One count carries √N.</strong> If you count only once and get N, your best estimate of the mean is N, and the spread you would see on repeating is about \\(\\sqrt N\\). Report \\(N \\pm \\sqrt N\\). The relative uncertainty \\(\\sqrt N/N = 1/\\sqrt N\\) shrinks as you count longer: {#} counts are good to {#} %, {#} to {#} %.",
"<strong>Waits are exponential.</strong> The time between one count and the next follows the <strong>exponential distribution</strong>, with density \\(f(t) = R\\,e^{-Rt}\\), mean \\(1/R\\) and standard deviation also \\(1/R\\). The share of waits longer than \\(t_0\\) is \\(P(T > t_0) = e^{-Rt_0}\\). The exponential is <strong>memoryless</strong>: if you have already waited \\(t_0\\) without a count, the time still to wait has the same exponential distribution, with the same mean \\(1/R\\), as if you had just started. A nucleus has no clock.",
"<strong>Goodness of fit.</strong> To test whether counts follow a given distribution, group the counts into cells, each expecting at least {#}, and compute",
"\\[\\chi^2 = \\sum \\frac{(O - E)^2}{E},\\]",
"with O the observed and E the expected number in each cell. If the counts follow the distribution, \\(\\chi^2\\) follows the chi square distribution with \\(df = \\text{cells} - 1\\) (μ is known here, not estimated); a large \\(\\chi^2\\) (a small p value) says they do not.",
